okay so now let's go from talking about  police to talking about destruction of  property there are reasonable people who  have centered in on what seems to be a  dividing line as we see unrest and  protests and physical violence and  property damage around the country when  we talk about property damage there is  often a debate as to whether it counts  as violence in the sense of when people  say I am against violence  so most people agree attacking random  bystanders who aren't being violent is  unacceptable violence attacking random  police who aren't doing anything wrong  that is violence in the in any sense of  the word  most people agree blocking traffic is  not violence now you could be for or  against blocking traffic as a protest  tactic but most people would not say  blocking traffic is violence you could  maybe say well if you prevent people  from getting to work and then they lose  their jobs blocking traffic becomes kind  of like economic violence but in the  kind of violence we're talking about  blocking traffic  most people agree is is not violence  what about property damage so first of  all we have to call out the right-wing  hypocrites on this issue the concern  trolls who a month ago were saying  sacrifice grandma to coronavirus in  order to open the economy who are now  saying the worst thing you can do is  break the windows of a big-box store  they are hypocrites I'm not interested  in those hypocritical concern trolls I  want to deal with the real people with  the sensible people who in a real way  want to talk about this there are folks  who believe that for example destroying  government property when no one gets  physically hurt is okay but for example  destroying a private business is not  okay that depends on who you're  targeting you could be targeting a  business with bad practices or you could  be targeting a government that is a bad  actor or whatever you could find it you  know a logging company and say we are  going to destroy or disable their  bulldozers so that they cannot take down  these trees saying what determines  whether destruction of property is  violence is merely whether you're doing  it to a government property or to  private property  I don't think that's really the dividing  line what  this comes down to is what do we mean by  violence violence defined as behavior  involving physical force to hurt damage  or kill someone or something damaging  properties  sort of fits if you want to take a legal  perspective certainly the unlawful  exercise of force or the exhibition of  such force would encompass the  destruction of property now others would  say the real crux of human violence  versus physical violence against people  rather than objects is where we should  be focusing if you are talking about  breaking a window that's not really what  a lot of people believe is violence so  here's the bottom line if you want to  make this a semantic argument if you  want to make this about definitions you  can define violence in a way that does  or doesn't include property crime it  won't really get to the underlying issue  and the underlying issue is whether  sometimes violence is justified so even  if we agree on whether property crime is  or is not violence that doesn't answer  the question of whether property crime  destruction of property is a valid form  of protest who gets to decide what's in  the protesting playbook if you leave the  protesting playbook only to the elites  and those in positions of power it will  be restricted to only writing letters  you know chanting and holding signs  right the point is if you let those  already in power define what is okay in  terms of protesting certainly nothing  will ever change now is there any  guarantee that destroying property is  going to achieve change there's not we  do know that destruction of property has  been a part of protesting for a really  long time you look at the Boston Tea  Party destroyed property to fight back  against the capitalist monopoly and  political rights that were being claimed  by British colonists you look at slave  uprisings in the United States which  included destruction of property you  look at the plowshares aid a group of  eight Christians including priests and  nuns who took hammers to the nose cones  some nuclear warheads poured their blood  on the facility's papers and a call for  peace  so the legality part of it is clear this  stuff is illegal to do but I think we  can all every single one of us imagine a  situation where we do the moderate  methods and they bring no change they  don't get attention and then you say  well we don't want to do violence to  people but we're going to start  destroying some property destruction of  property in my mind is clearly violence  of a kind but in certain situations it  might be the only thing people have left  short of starting to actually do  violence to individuals now which  situations count I don't know I don't  know what about the consequences well  there are consequences you're not going  to get around that will property damage  get you what you want often it will not  property damage may in fact do the  opposite it may turn public opinion  against you but whether property damage  that's not hurting anyone physically is  a valid form of protest that's a  question that goes back at least  hundreds of years and anybody pretending  that there is one single solitary answer  that applies to every situation is just  wrong history tells us that's not the  case I want to hear from you about this  we're going to continue covering it on  the David Pakman show  Instagram at David Pakman show we will  take a break and be back with much more
 

